{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reclassifies small-scale possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs may face up to six months in jail or be offered an alternative: enrollment in a behavioral health initiative and a diversion pathway designed to avoid fines. The law also tightens restrictions on sales near areas like playgrounds, signaling a shift toward stricter enforcement.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now expected to assess each case quickly. If a person has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable, officers can contact a local behavioral health dispatch line. A mobile team must arrive within half an hour to evaluate and offer support. If they don’t, the individual will be taken into custody—there’s no option to delay or wait indefinitely. This rapid-response model reflects the urgency law enforcement feels in balancing public safety with individual needs.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue that giving counties flexibility allows them to tailor responses based on available capacity and local priorities. Critics, however, warn this could lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions, where access to services varies widely. Some fear the narrow eligibility rules will exclude many who need help most, turning what was once a compassionate approach into a more punitive one.\n\n***\n\nThe shift marks a turning point in how one state handles addiction, contrasting with broader global trends. In some parts of the country, people over a certain age are still being jailed for possession, while elsewhere, systems prioritize care over confinement. Imagine if the same logic applied to shopping—where hunger meant arrest instead of food. The tongue of policy now speaks louder than ever before.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.9851841926574707}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.02703193575143814}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.9216428995132446}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.1843518614768982}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -6.5205356804654e-05}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.8981673121452332}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.030287884175777435}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -3.1709168979432434e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.002969976281747222}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.03804164379835129}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -5.924526340095326e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " meth", "logprob": -1.549708758830093e-05}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.009765825234353542}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.04140680655837059}, {"token": " misdemeanor", "logpr